  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "The postposition 'को' is used in many ways in Hindi. It often shows how a noun, pronoun, or phrase relates to the verb, particularly when there is an indirect object. The indirect object is always marked by 'को'. For example, 'मुझको नमक दीजिये' means 'Please pass me the salt', and 'हमको सड़क दिखाइये' means 'Please show us the way'. The short forms 'मुझे', 'हमें', 'उन्हें', etc., can be used interchangeably. An indirect object is used in 'अपने पिता जी को चिट्ठी भेज रहा हूँ' meaning 'I am sending a letter to my father'.\nAdverbial phrases using 'के लिये' (for) indicate purpose or for whom something is intended. Examples include 'मेरे लिये' for 'for me', 'हमारे लिये' for 'for us', 'आपके लिये' for 'for your sake', 'इन के लिये' for 'for them/for him/for her', 'इस के लिये' for 'for him/for her' (singular), and 'किस के लिये' meaning 'for whom?'.\nThis construction is also used with nouns, particularly verb infinitives, to express purpose. For example, 'खाना खाने के लिये चलिये' means 'Let’s go to eat'. Before verbs such as 'आ' (to come), 'जा' (to go), and 'चल' (to walk/go), 'के लिये' may be omitted, but the infinitive remains in the oblique case. An example is 'सिनेमा देखने जा रहे थे', meaning 'They were going to watch a movie'.\n"
    }
